# Softverse

**Stata is the language social science replication code is written in, and it
is the language nobody counts.** Across __N_ANALYZABLE__ deposits with analyzable code at
journals whose data-and-code policy an editor verifies, __N_STATA__ contain Stata
and __N_R__ contain R. Studies of research software routinely cover R and Python
and stop there, which describes a field while claiming to describe social
science.

The second finding is what the counts credit. The most used Stata package is
`__TOP_STATA__`, in __TOP_STATA_N__ deposits, and __N_FORMATTERS__ of the four most
used exist to format regression output. The software that gets loaded most is the software that
turns results into tables.

The third is what no registry can see. `grc1leg` is called __N_GRC1LEG__ times in this
corpus and belongs to no package archive; it is distributed from StataCorp's
own site. A credit system built on registries is blind to it.

## Why count this way

Credit is what gets software written. The metrics available are weak: a
download counts a fetch and rewards frequent releases, and a mention in prose
counts what an author remembered to name. Neither says the software ran.

*Validated use* is the stronger claim. A package was loaded by code deposited
with a published paper, at a journal whose data-and-code policy the Social
Science Data Editors record as actively verified. That is a criterion a reader
can check.

## Three ways in

**<a href="lookup/">Look up a package</a>.** Every one of the __N_PACKAGES__ packages,
searchable, with the number of deposits that load it and the denominator it is
a share of. A search that finds nothing in the registries still answers: type
`grc1leg` and it reports the calls and says no registry indexes it.

**<a href="paper/softverse.pdf">Read the paper</a>.** The frame, the measurement, and the
validation, including the R extractor measured against R's own parser.

**<a href="data/">Take the data</a>.** The per-package counts as CSV under CC0, plus the
unresolved names and a datapackage descriptor. The Stata command-to-package
index is deposited separately at
[10.5281/zenodo.21926100](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21926100), because
anyone counting Stata needs the same mapping whether or not they care about
this corpus.

## What the counts are not

Static reference in deposited code. A package loaded in a superseded script or
a dead branch still counts, and code kept out of the deposit is invisible. The
per-package counts cover the Zenodo half of the frame, which is economics;
Harvard Dataverse's journal collections are political science and are tallied
separately.

